  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nEarly morning water vapor analysis shows quite nicely low pressure \nspinning over the Canadian Prairie, with persistent SSW flow into \nthe Great Lakes out ahead of it advecting a moisture-rich airmass \ninto the area. Polar satellite imagery indicates PWATs already near \nan inch over the UP, and higher values up to 1.75in over IA and MN. \nMeanwhile, spotty showers are already reaching the western UP so \nfar, but a more widespread batch of showers (with embedded thunder) \nis moving northward into east-central MN on the nose of a stronger \n~45kt LLJ core. This next batch looks to begin to spread into the \nwestern UP during the pre-dawn hours, continuing eastward through \nthe morning and afternoon. Will note that CAMS continue to show \nshower activity becoming more spotty with eastward extent, then \nshowers finally taper off in the eastern UP late tonight into early \nWednesday morning as the cold front passes through. \n\nMost of the area looks to receive at a wetting rainfall. The latest \nforecast reflects a widespread 0.10-0.25in over the eastern half of \nthe UP, and 0.50-0.75in over the western half through this evening. \nHowever, NBM still shows a 50-60% chance for higher embedded totals \nin excess of an inch over the far western UP given the potential for \nthunderstorms and heavier downpours this morning. A good wind gust \ndoesn't seem out of the question in any storms with such strong flow \naloft, and will note that plenty of sites out in eastern MN are \nshowing gusts to around 30mph in storms. However, we remain under a \nsteep capping inversion given the WAA aloft, so confidence in the \ndevelopment of particularly strong to severe storms is low. \n\nGusty non-thunderstorm winds also remain a concern for today and \nWednesday. The strongest gusts in this southerly warm advection \nregime will be in the areas favored by southerly downslope flow, \nparticularly from the Huron Mountains eastward along the Lake \nSuperior shoreline. These areas show a 60-70% chance for wind gusts \nof 35+ mph through this evening per latest HREF and NBM guidance.\nWinds shift westerly and strengthen further on Wednesday, aided by \ncold air advection and pressure rises behind the cold front. Expect \nwidespread gusts of 20-30 mph through the day, with NBM \nprobabilities now increasing to 30-60% for gusts to 40 mph near Lake \nSuperior. A Wind Advisory for the Keweenaw continues to look like a \nstrong consideration as the NBM probability for gusts of 45+ mph has \nincreased to 70-90%. Meanwhile, will also have to watch areas to the \neast as there is a 25-40% probability for 45 mph gusts along the \nshoreline from east of Marquette to Whitefish Point.\n\nExpect calmer weather to return to the region by Thursday as high \npressure temporarily builds back over the UP behind the departing \nsystem, with highs on Thursday closer to seasonal norms in the mid \n60s to low 70s. However, the overall pattern remains unsettled as \nanother trough has the potential to bring additional rounds of \nprecipitation and breezy conditions into the weekend.",
